{
  "requirement": "Each invocation adds the UTF-8 byte length of its result text to the tool’s `result_bytes`, measured before the provider encodes the request. Error messages count by the same rule. An invocation that produces no result text adds zero bytes.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The two tests cover each obligation in `requirements.sdk.graph.running-criteria.ToolUsage.result_bytes`.\n\n**UTF-8 byte length (a sum per tool).** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs#fn[tool_usage_counts_utf8_results_and_errors_once_across_retries]` uses a fixture `MinimalConversation::dispatch`. For `read_node` it returns `\"λ\".repeat(320)`, which is 320 characters but 640 UTF-8 bytes. The test makes two `read_node` calls and asserts `result_bytes` of 1280. A character count (640) or a single call (640) would fail that assertion. The assertion also shows bytes add up across invocations.\n\n**Error messages count by the same rule.** The `lookup` call with key \"missing\" makes the fixture return `io::Error::other(\"absent\")`. The test asserts `lookup` has `result_bytes` of 6, which is the length of the error text. An implementation that ignored errors would fail. The error text is ASCII only, so it doesn't show multi-byte counting for errors. Error and success text go through the same dispatch result path, so I did not treat this as a gap.\n\n**No result text adds zero.** `submit_result` is invoked once. The test asserts `{\"calls\":1,\"result_bytes\":0}`. This checks that an invocation with no result text adds zero.\n\n**Counted once across retries.** A throttled provider error sits between the first tool turn and the final turn, so the request is retried. The totals stay exact (2 calls, 1280 bytes), so nothing is double counted.\n\n**Real tool plumbing.** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[registered_tools_use_the_current_context_and_ordinary_node_ids]` is marked {test} for this requirement. It checks `result_bytes` against the actual function-call output strings sent back to the provider. It does this for `lookup_requirements`, whose two calls include an error output containing \"invalid type\", and for `inspect`. Each is compared with the byte length of the output text, `output(n).len()`. This covers real registered tools, including an error result.\n\nNo acknowledgments apply, and I found no unaddressed gaps.",
